::GEO_U04_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar rectangles have corresponding side lengths 5 and 15. What is the scale factor from the smaller rectangle to the larger rectangle? {
=A. 3#The scale factor is 15 divided by 5, which equals 3.
~B. &frac13;#This is the reverse scale factor from larger to smaller.
~C. 10#This subtracts the side lengths instead of comparing them multiplicatively.
~D. 20#This adds the side lengths and does not describe scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has side 8 corresponding to side 12 in a similar triangle. If another side is 10, what is the corresponding side length in the larger triangle? {
~A. 14#Adding 4 repeats the first side difference, but similar figures use multiplication by scale factor.
=B. 15#The scale factor is <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub> = 1.5, and 10(1.5) = 15.
~C. 6#This uses the inverse relationship instead of scaling up.
~D. 18#This does not use the scale factor 1.5 from the corresponding sides.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two congruent figures are also similar. What is their scale factor? {
~A. 0#A scale factor of 0 would collapse lengths and cannot describe congruent figures.
~B. 2#A scale factor of 2 changes size, so the figures would not be congruent.
=C. 1#Congruent figures have equal corresponding lengths, so the scale factor is 1.
~D. &frac12;#A scale factor of &frac12; changes size, so the figures would not be congruent.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A map scale says 1 inch represents 20 miles. If two towns are 3 inches apart on the map, how far apart are they in reality? {
~A. 23 miles#Adding 3 and 20 does not use the scale relationship.
~B. 6.7 miles#Dividing 20 by 3 reverses the map-scale relationship.
~C. 40 miles#This would match 2 inches, not 3 inches.
=D. 60 miles#Use the scale proportion: 3 inches represents 3(20) = 60 miles.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A 6-foot person casts a 9-foot shadow. A nearby flagpole casts a 27-foot shadow. Assuming similar triangles, how tall is the flagpole? {
=A. 18 feet#The shadow scale factor is <sup>27</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub> = 3, so the height is 6(3) = 18 feet.
~B. 12 feet#This does not use the scale factor from the shadows.
~C. 30 feet#Adding 21 to the height copies a difference rather than using proportional reasoning.
~D. 40.5 feet#Multiplying 27 by 1.5 does not match the person-shadow ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which proportion correctly solves for x when corresponding sides are 4 to 10 and 6 to x? {
~A. <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = <sup>10</sup>&frasl;<sub>x</sub>#This compares sides from different figures in the first ratio and can mismatch correspondence.
=B. <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub> = <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>x</sub>#Corresponding sides must be matched in the same order in both ratios.
~C. 4 + 10 = 6 + x#Similar figures require multiplicative ratios, not additive equations.
~D. 4x = 6#This omits the corresponding side length 10 and does not represent the proportion.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A scale drawing uses scale factor &frac14; from the actual object to the drawing. An actual length is 28 cm. What is the drawing length? {
~A. 24 cm#Subtracting 4 does not use the scale factor.
~B. 32 cm#Adding 4 does not use proportional scaling.
=C. 7 cm#Multiply by the scale factor: 28(&frac14;) = 7.
~D. 112 cm#This uses the reciprocal scale factor and scales up instead of down.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar polygons have scale factor 2 from Figure A to Figure B. If a side in Figure A is 11, what is the corresponding side in Figure B? {
~A. 13#Adding 2 uses the scale factor as an amount to add, not a multiplier.
~B. 9#Subtracting 2 does not describe similarity scaling.
~C. 5.5#This incorrectly divides by the scale factor.
=D. 22#A scale factor of 2 doubles each corresponding side length.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which conclusion is valid if all corresponding sides of two quadrilaterals are proportional and corresponding angles are congruent? {
=A. The quadrilaterals are similar.#Congruent corresponding angles and proportional corresponding sides support similarity.
~B. The quadrilaterals must be congruent.#Similarity allows different sizes; congruence would require scale factor 1.
~C. The quadrilaterals cannot be compared.#The given correspondence provides enough evidence to compare them.
~D. Only one pair of sides is related.#The statement says all corresponding sides are proportional.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A model car is built at a scale of 1:18. If the model is 10 cm long, what is the actual car length? {
~A. 28 cm#Adding 18 to 10 does not use the scale ratio.
=B. 180 cm#A 1:18 scale means the actual length is 18 times the model length, so 10(18) = 180 cm.
~C. 8 cm#Subtracting does not represent scale.
~D. 1.8 cm#Dividing by 18 scales in the wrong direction.
}

